uction often uses a name and です. Personal names are not a reason to ask for legal identity details.","objectives":["Say a chosen name","Ask someone's name politely","Express a simple preference"],"grammar":"A は B です introduces a topic and a polite statement; は is read wa here. In ねこが好きです, が marks what is liked. Subjects can be left unstated when the context is clear. です is not a universal translation of every English 'is'.","writing":"Optional: explore Kana names and the particles は and が in short sentences.","roles":[["Morning Routine"],["Family"],["School & Work"],["Everyday Actions"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["はじめまして。","Nice to meet you (first meeting).","Hajimemashite."],["アキです。","I am Aki.","Aki desu."],["お名前は何ですか。","What is your name?","Onamae wa nan desu ka."],["よろしくお願いします。","I look forward to getting to know you.","Yoroshiku onegaishimasu."],["ねこが好きです。","I like cats.","Neko ga suki desu."],["私もです。","Me too.","Watashi mo desu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"Aki introduces themself. You want to ask your next guide's name politely. Choose your question.","answer":"お名前は何ですか。","choices":["お名前は何ですか。","ねこが好きです。","私もです。"],"explanation":"お名前は何ですか asks someone's name; the other choices express a preference or agreement."},"source":"tokyo-guide"},"Polite Expressions":{"slug":"tokyo-greetings","location":"Welcome to Tokyo","aliases":["Greetings & Introductions"],"culture":"Tokyo is a setting for your first everyday exchanges. Choose greetings for the time of day and the relationship; there is no single greeting for every situation.","objectives":["Greet someone during the day and in the morning","Get attention politely","Close a short exchange with thanks"],"grammar":"ございます makes おはようございます a polite morning greeting. すみません can get someone's attention or express an apology. Keep the long vowel in ありがとう; romanization is a reading aid, not English spelling.","writing":"Optional: try Hiragana vowels and notice how こんにちは is written with は, pronounced wa in this greeting.","roles":[["Saying Hello","Hello and goodbye"],["Introducing Yourself"],["Asking Someone's Name"],["Basic Conversation"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["こんにちは","Hello (daytime)","konnichiwa"],["おはようございます","Good morning (polite)","ohayō gozaimasu"],["こんばんは","Good evening","konbanwa"],["すみません","Excuse me / sorry","sumimasen"],["ありがとうございます","Thank you (polite)","arigatō gozaimasu"],["またね","See you (casual)","mata ne"]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You meet your guide at nine in the morning. Which polite greeting fits?","answer":"おはようございます","choices":["おはようございます","こんばんは","またね"],"explanation":"おはようございます is a polite morning greeting. こんばんは is used in the evening; またね is a casual farewell."},"source":"tokyo-greetings"},"Basic Conversation":{"slug":"tokyo-guide","location":"Meet Your Tokyo Guide","aliases":["Daily Life"],"culture":"Your guide's name is Aki in this fictional conversation. A short self-introduction often uses a name and です. Personal names are not a reason to ask for legal identity details.","objectives":["Say a chosen name","Ask someone's name politely","Express a simple preference"],"grammar":"A は B です introduces a topic and a polite statement; は is read wa here. In ねこが好きです, が marks what is liked. Subjects can be left unstated when the context is clear. です is not a universal translation of every English 'is'.","writing":"Optional: explore Kana names and the particles は and が in short sentences.","roles":[["Morning Routine"],["Family"],["School & Work"],["Everyday Actions"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["はじめまして。","Nice to meet you (first meeting).","Hajimemashite."],["アキです。","I am Aki.","Aki desu."],["お名前は何ですか。","What is your name?","Onamae wa nan desu ka."],["よろしくお願いします。","I look forward to getting to know you.","Yoroshiku onegaishimasu."],["ねこが好きです。","I like cats.","Neko ga suki desu."],["私もです。","Me too.","Watashi mo desu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"Aki introduces themself. You want to ask your next guide's name politely. Choose your question.","answer":"お名前は何ですか。","choices":["お名前は何ですか。","ねこが好きです。","私もです。"],"explanation":"お名前は何ですか asks someone's name; the other choices express a preference or agreement."},"source":"tokyo-guide"},"Asking for Help":{"slug":"tokyo-station","location":"Tokyo Train Station","aliases":["Around the City"],"culture":"Large stations can have many exits. Ask a staff member when unsure, and read the destination as well as the platform. These directional phrases are practice, not real navigation instructions.","objectives":["Ask where a station is","Recognize left and right","Ask someone to repeat slowly"],"grammar":"Place + はどこですか asks where something is. 右 (migi) is right and 左 (hidari) is left. もう一度お願いします is a useful repair phrase when you do not understand the first answer.","writing":"Optional: recognize 駅 on signs and compare 右 and 左.","roles":[["Places in Town","Places to go"],["Asking Where Something Is"],["Directions"],["Finding a Destination"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["駅","Station","eki"],["駅はどこですか。","Where is the station?","Eki wa doko desu ka."],["右です。","It is on the right.","Migi desu."],["左です。","It is on the left.","Hidari desu."],["まっすぐです。","It is straight ahead.","Massugu desu."],["もう一度お願いします。","Once more, please.","Mō ichido onegaishimasu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You missed the staff member's answer. What can you say to ask for it again?","answer":"もう一度お願いします。","choices":["もう一度お願いします。","右です。","駅"],"explanation":"もう一度お願いします asks for repetition; 右です gives a direction rather than asking for clarification."},"source":"tokyo-station"},"Transportation":{"slug":"tokyo-station","location":"Tokyo Train Station","aliases":["Around the City"],"culture":"Large stations can have many exits. Ask a staff member when unsure, and read the destination as well as the platform. These directional phrases are practice, not real navigation instructions.","objectives":["Ask where a station is","Recognize left and right","Ask someone to repeat slowly"],"grammar":"Place + はどこですか asks where something is. 右 (migi) is right and 左