 too.","Watashi mo desu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"Aki introduces themself. You want to ask your next guide's name politely. Choose your question.","answer":"お名前は何ですか。","choices":["お名前は何ですか。","ねこが好きです。","私もです。"],"explanation":"お名前は何ですか asks someone's name; the other choices express a preference or agreement."},"source":"tokyo-guide"},"City Challenge":{"slug":"tokyo-station","location":"Tokyo Train Station","aliases":["Around the City"],"culture":"Large stations can have many exits. Ask a staff member when unsure, and read the destination as well as the platform. These directional phrases are practice, not real navigation instructions.","objectives":["Ask where a station is","Recognize left and right","Ask someone to repeat slowly"],"grammar":"Place + はどこですか asks where something is. 右 (migi) is right and 左 (hidari) is left. もう一度お願いします is a useful repair phrase when you do not understand the first answer.","writing":"Optional: recognize 駅 on signs and compare 右 and 左.","roles":[["Places in Town","Places to go"],["Asking Where Something Is"],["Directions"],["Finding a Destination"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["駅","Station","eki"],["駅はどこですか。","Where is the station?","Eki wa doko desu ka."],["右です。","It is on the right.","Migi desu."],["左です。","It is on the left.","Hidari desu."],["まっすぐです。","It is straight ahead.","Massugu desu."],["もう一度お願いします。","Once more, please.","Mō ichido onegaishimasu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You missed the staff member's answer. What can you say to ask for it again?","answer":"もう一度お願いします。","choices":["もう一度お願いします。","右です。","駅"],"explanation":"もう一度お願いします asks for repetition; 右です gives a direction rather than asking for clarification."},"source":"tokyo-station"},"Numbers & Money":{"slug":"tokyo-numbers","location":"Tokyo Convenience Store","aliases":["Numbers & Time"],"culture":"A convenience store gives you a reason to recognize small quantities and prices. Prices in this adventure are fictional practice examples, not current shop prices.","objectives":["Recognize one, two and three","Ask a price","Read a simple time expression"],"grammar":"いくらですか asks how much something costs. 円 is the currency counter; pronounce 百円 as hyaku-en. Time uses 時: 三時です means it is three o'clock. Counters change how numbers are used, so learn the whole expression.","writing":"Optional: recognize 一, 二 and 三. Say their readings in the phrases instead of assuming every Kanji has only one reading.","roles":[["Numbers 1–10","Numbers 1-10","Your first numbers"],["Numbers 11–100","Numbers 11-100"],["Asking the Time"],["Dates"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["一","One","ichi"],["二","Two","ni"],["三","Three","san"],["いくらですか。","How much is it?","Ikura desu ka."],["百円です。","It is one hundred yen.","Hyaku-en desu."],["三時です。","It is three o'clock.","San-ji desu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You can see the item you want, but cannot find its price. What do you ask?","answer":"いくらですか。","choices":["いくらですか。","三時です。","二"],"explanation":"いくらですか asks a price. 三時です states a time, and 二 is the number two."},"source":"tokyo-numbers"},"Numbers 11–100":{"slug":"tokyo-numbers","location":"Tokyo Convenience Store","aliases":["Numbers & Time"],"culture":"A convenience store gives you a reason to recognize small quantities and prices. Prices in this adventure are fictional practice examples, not current shop prices.","objectives":["Recognize one, two and three","Ask a price","Read a simple time expression"],"grammar":"Build eleven as 十一 and twelve as 十二. 二十 is two tens, or twenty. 百 is one hundred. Learn the readings along with the written numbers.","writing":"Optional: recognize 一, 二 and 三. Say their readings in the phrases instead of assuming every Kanji has only one reading.","roles":[["Numbers 1–10","Numbers 1-10","Your first numbers"],["Numbers 11–100","Numbers 11-100"],["Asking the Time"],["Dates"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["十一","Eleven","jūichi"],["十二","Twelve","jūni"],["二十","Twenty","nijū"],["三十","Thirty","sanjū"],["百","One hundred","hyaku"]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You can see the item you want, but cannot find its price. What do you ask?","answer":"いくらですか。","choices":["いくらですか。","三時です。","二"],"explanation":"いくらですか asks a price. 三時です states a time, and 二 is the number two."},"source":"tokyo-numbers"},"Asking the Time":{"slug":"tokyo-numbers","location":"Tokyo Convenience Store","aliases":["Numbers & Time"],"culture":"A convenience store gives you a reason to recognize small quantities and prices. Prices in this adventure are fictional practice examples, not current shop prices.","objectives":["Recognize one, two and three","Ask a price","Read a simple time expression"],"grammar":"何時ですか asks the time. 時 follows the hour number. Four o'clock uses よじ (yo-ji), not yon-ji; seven o'clock commonly uses shichi-ji. Read each whole time expression.","writing":"Optional: recognize 一, 二 and 三. Say their readings in the phrases instead of assuming every Kanji has only one reading.","roles":[["Numbers 1–10","Numbers 1-10","Your first numbers"],["Numbers 11–100","Numbers 11-100"],["Asking the Time"],["Dates"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["何時ですか。","What time is it?","Nan-ji desu ka."],["三時です。","It is three o'clock.","San-ji desu."],["四時です。","It is four o'clock.","Yo-ji desu."],["七時です。","It is seven o'clock.","Shichi-ji desu."],["三時半です。","It is half past three.","San-ji han desu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You can see the item you want, but cannot find its price. What do you ask?","answer":"いくらですか。","choices":["いくらですか。","三時です。","二"],"explanation":"いくらですか asks a price. 三時です states a time, and 二 is the number two."},"source":"tokyo-numbers"},"Days of the Week":{"slug":"tokyo-numbers","location":"Tokyo Convenience Store","aliases":["Numbers & Time"],"culture":"A convenience store gives you a reason to recognize small quantities and prices. Prices in this adventure are fictional practice examples, not current shop prices.","objectives":["Recognize one, two and three","Ask a price","Read a simple time expression"],"grammar":"The weekday names end in 曜日 (yōbi). Ask 何曜日ですか to find out which day of the week it is. Learn the names as whole words.","writing":"Optional: recognize 一, 二 and 三. Say their readings in the phrases instead of assuming every Kanji has only one reading.","roles":[["Numbers 1–10","Numbers 1-10","Your first numbers"],["Numbers 11–100","Numbers 11-100"],["Asking the Time"],["Dates"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["月曜日","Monday","getsuyōbi"],["火曜日","Tuesday","kayōbi"],["水曜日","Wednesday","suiyōbi"],["木曜日","Thursday","mokuyōbi"],["金曜日","Friday","kin'yōbi"],["土曜日","Saturday","doyōbi"],["日曜日","Sunday","nichiyōbi"]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You can see the item you want, but cannot find its price. What do you ask?","answer":"いくらですか。","choices":["いくらですか。","三時です。","二"],"explanation":"いくらですか asks a price. 三時です states a time, and 二 is the number two."},"source":"tokyo-numbers"},"Dates":{"slug":"tokyo-numbers","location":"Tokyo Convenience Store","aliases":["Numbers & Time"],"culture":"A convenience store gives you a reason to recognize small quantities and prices. Prices in this adventure are fictional practice examples, not current shop prices.","objectives":["Recognize one, two and three","Ask a price","Read a simple time expression"],"grammar":"Calendar dates have special readings. 一日 is tsuitachi for the first day of a month and 二日 is futsuka. 月 after a number gives the month; 一月 is January.","writing":"Optional: recognize 一, 二 and 三. Say their readings in the phrases instead of assuming every Kanji has only one reading.","roles":[["Numbers 1–10","Numbers 1-10","Your first numbers"],["Numbers 11–100","Numbers 11-100"],["Asking the Time"],["Dates"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["一月","January","ichigatsu"],["二月","February","nigatsu"],["一日","The first day of a month","tsuitachi"],["二日","The second day of a month","futsuka"],["今日","Today","kyō"]],"scenario":{"prompt":"You can see the item you want, but cannot find its price. What do you ask?","answer":"いくらですか。","choices":["いくらですか。","三時です。","二"],"explanation":"いくらですか asks a price. 三時です states a time, and 二 is the number two."},"source":"tokyo-numbers"},"Morning Routine":{"slug":"tokyo-guide","location":"Meet Your Tokyo Guide","aliases":["Daily Life"],"culture":"Your guide's name is Aki in this fictional conversation. A short self-introduction often uses a name and です. Personal names are not a reason to ask for legal identity details.","objectives":["Say a chosen name","Ask someone's name politely","Express a simple preference"],"grammar":"Use a verb ending in ます for a polite statement about an action. 朝 means morning. Read the whole expressions before trying to change their tense.","writing":"Optional: explore Kana names and the particles は and が in short sentences.","roles":[["Morning Routine"],["Family"],["School & Work"],["Everyday Actions"],["Unit Review"]],"words":[["朝","Morning","asa"],["起きます。","I get up.","Okimasu."],["食べます。","I eat.","Tabemasu."],["飲みます。","I drink.","Nomimasu."],["行きます。","I go.","Ikimasu."]],"scenario":{"prompt":"Aki introduces themself. You want to ask your next guide's name politely. Choose your question.","answer":"お名前は何ですか。","choices":["お名前は何ですか。","ねこが好きです。","私もです。"],"explanation":"お名前は何ですか asks someone's name; the other choices express a preference or agreement."},"source":"tokyo-guide"},"Family":{"slug":"tokyo-guide","location":"Meet Your Tokyo Guide","aliases":["Daily Life"],"culture":"Your guide's name is Aki in this fictional conversation. A short self-introduction often uses a name and です. Personal names are not a reason to ask for legal identity details.","objectives":["Say a chosen name","Ask someone's name politely","Express a simple preference"],"grammar":"Japanese family words depend on whose family you mean. These examples describe your own family to someone outside it. Father and mother are 父 and 母 in that context; do not assume these are forms of address.","writing":"Optional: explore Kana names and the particles は and が in short sentences.","roles":[["Morning Routine"],["Family"],["School 